'''Elastòmers de Balboa, S.A.''' ({{lang|ca|Elastòmers de Balboa}}), commonly abbreviated '''EDB''', is a Balboan {{w|synthetic rubber}} manufacturer headquartered in [[Sant Bart]], [[Jala Province]]. The company is the sole domestic producer of {{w|nitrile rubber|acrylonitrile butadiene rubber (NBR)}} and {{w|polybutadiene|butadiene rubber (BR)}} in Balboa, supplying the domestic automotive, industrial, and consumer goods sectors as well as export markets across the [[BTPMEU]] and beyond. EDB operates a research and development laboratory in [[Sant Cristòfor]] and its main manufacturing complex at [[Badia Curta]], adjacent to the [[CIEA]] refinery and [[Polímers de Balboa|PDB]] resin complex, which supply the petrochemical feedstocks required for synthetic rubber production.

EDB is listed on the [[Bolsa de Sant Cristòfor]] (SCX: EDB) and is a constituent of the [[SCX 100]] index.

== History ==

=== Founding (1991) ===

EDB was founded in 1991 by '''Jordi Aragó i Vidal''', a chemical engineer who had previously worked at [[CIEA]]'s Badia Curta refinery as a process engineer, and '''Marta Català i Soler''', a materials scientist who had studied elastomer formulations at the University of Sant Cristòfor. The two recognised that Balboa's growing automotive and industrial manufacturing sectors were entirely dependent on imported synthetic rubber from Volisania and Breisland, despite the country having domestic access to the key feedstocks — butadiene and acrylonitrile — through CIEA's refining operations and the emerging petrochemical cluster at Badia Curta.

The company was established as a private venture with backing from a group of Sant Bart investors and a feedstock supply agreement with CIEA, which saw strategic value in a domestic customer for its butadiene stream. The choice of Sant Bart as headquarters reflected the founders' connections to the city and its position as a growing industrial centre in the Jala highlands, while the manufacturing facility was located at Badia Curta to be adjacent to the feedstock source.

=== Early operations and listing (1991–2000) ===

EDB's Badia Curta plant began production in 1993, initially producing butadiene rubber (BR) for the domestic market. The company's first customers were domestic manufacturers of industrial hoses, belts, and gaskets, who had previously relied on imports. The [[Règim de compensació a l'exportació|negative tariff]] on manufactured exports, established in 1981, made EDB's products competitive for export to BTPMEU markets, and the company began exporting to Tapuya and Potocsí by the mid-1990s.

NBR production was added in 1996, targeting the automotive sector, which required oil-resistant rubber for seals, gaskets, and fuel system components. The addition of NBR capacity required investment in acrylonitrile handling and polymerisation technology, which was sourced from a Volisanian licensor under a technology transfer agreement.

EDB was listed on the [[Bolsa de Sant Cristòfor]] in 1998, raising capital to expand both BR and NBR production capacity. The [[Fons d'Inversió Directa de Balboa]] (FIDB) acquired an 8 per cent stake at the time of the listing, and CIEA acquired a 5 per cent stake, formalising the feedstock supply relationship. The [[Balboa Pension Fund]] (BPF) acquired a 5 per cent stake as an institutional anchor investor.

=== Expansion (2000–present) ===

The establishment of the [[BTPMEU]] in 1998 opened the Tapuya and Potocsí markets for EDB's products, and the company expanded its export sales through the 2000s. The automotive supply chain was a key growth driver, as the assembly sector in Balboa and the BTPMEU required increasing quantities of synthetic rubber components. EDB developed specialty NBR formulations for the automotive industry, including high-temperature resistant grades for engine compartments and fuel system seals.

In 2007, EDB opened a research and development laboratory in [[Sant Cristòfor]], in the Dàrsena industrial district, to develop new elastomer formulations and improve production processes. The Sant Cristòfor location provided access to the university research community and the concentration of industrial companies in the canal corridor. The laboratory has since become the company's primary centre for product development, working on next-generation NBR grades, BR with improved wear resistance, and specialty blends for the semiconductor and medical equipment sectors.

The company expanded its Badia Curta manufacturing complex in 2015, adding a second NBR polymerisation line and increasing total production capacity by approximately 40 per cent. The expansion was driven by growing demand from the Balboan automotive sector and increased exports to [[Balisca]] and [[Okaiken]].

== Operations ==

EDB operates two facilities:

* '''Badia Curta manufacturing complex''' ([[Jala Province]]): The company's sole production facility, located adjacent to the [[CIEA]] refinery and [[Polímers de Balboa|PDB]] resin complex. The complex includes BR and NBR polymerisation units, compounding and mixing lines, quality control laboratories, and packaging and storage facilities. Butadiene is supplied by pipeline from CIEA's refinery, while acrylonitrile is delivered by road tanker from CIEA's chemical processing unit. The complex employs approximately 1,100 people.

* '''Sant Cristòfor R&D laboratory''' ([[Sant Cristòfor]]): The company's research and development centre, located in the Dàrsena industrial district. The laboratory develops new elastomer formulations, conducts product testing and certification, and works on process improvement. The laboratory employs approximately 100 people.

The company's headquarters functions are based in [[Sant Bart]], employing approximately 200 people in administration, finance, sales, and management.

== Products ==

EDB produces two principal synthetic rubber product lines:

=== Acrylonitrile butadiene rubber (NBR) ===

{{w|Nitrile rubber|NBR}} is a synthetic rubber copolymer of acrylonitrile and butadiene, valued for its oil resistance, fuel resistance, and mechanical strength. EDB produces NBR in several acrylonitrile content grades:

* '''Low ACN grades''' (18–22% acrylonitrile): Applications requiring flexibility at low temperatures, such as refrigeration seals and gaskets.
* '''Medium ACN grades''' (28–34% acrylonitrile): General-purpose oil-resistant applications, including automotive seals, fuel hoses, gaskets, and industrial gloves.
* '''High ACN grades''' (40–50% acrylonitrile): Applications requiring maximum oil and fuel resistance, such as fuel system components, hydraulic seals, and oil field equipment.

NBR accounts for approximately 60 per cent of EDB's revenue.

=== Butadiene rubber (BR) ===

{{w|Polybutadiene|BR}} is a synthetic rubber homopolymer of butadiene, characterised by high abrasion resistance, low heat build-up, and good flexibility at low temperatures. EDB produces BR primarily for:

* '''Tyre manufacturing''': Blended with natural rubber and other synthetic rubbers to improve wear resistance and rolling resistance. EDB supplies BR to domestic and BTPMEU tyre manufacturers.
* '''Industrial products''': Conveyor belts, industrial hoses, and vibration dampeners requiring high abrasion resistance.
* '''Golf ball cores''': High-resilience BR grades for golf ball manufacturing, exported to sporting goods manufacturers in [[Balisca]] and [[Okaiken]].

BR accounts for approximately 30 per cent of EDB's revenue.

=== Specialty blends ===

EDB produces custom NBR/BR blends and specialty elastomer compounds for specific customer requirements, including:

* '''NBR/PVC blends''': Improved ozone and weather resistance for outdoor applications.
* '''Hydrogenated NBR (HNBR)''': High-temperature resistant grades for automotive engine compartment applications.
* '''Custom compounds''': Formulated to customer specifications for specific industrial applications.

Specialty blends account for approximately 10 per cent of EDB's revenue.

== Feedstock supply ==

EDB's manufacturing complex at Badia Curta is integrated with the local petrochemical cluster:

* '''Butadiene''': Extracted from CIEA's refinery operations and supplied by pipeline directly to EDB's polymerisation units. CIEA's 5 per cent stake in EDB reflects the strategic importance of this supply relationship.
* '''Acrylonitrile''': Produced from propylene and ammonia at CIEA's chemical processing unit, delivered to EDB by road tanker. The overvalued International Dollar makes imported propylene and ammonia relatively cheap for CIEA, which passes on the cost advantage to EDB.
* '''Electricity''': Supplied by the [[Yalabirga]] hydropower system, providing low-cost power for the energy-intensive polymerisation process.
* '''Water''': Drawn from the Badia Curta municipal water supply, with on-site treatment and recycling facilities.

The [[Règim de compensació a l'exportació|negative tariff]] on manufactured exports offsets the overvalued ID's cost penalty on EDB's export sales, making the company's products competitive in BTPMEU and international markets.

== Ownership ==

EDB is publicly traded on the [[Bolsa de Sant Cristòfor]]. The ownership structure reflects the company's history as a private venture that secured strategic industrial partnerships:

* '''[[Fons d'Inversió Directa de Balboa]] (FIDB)''': 8% — acquired at the time of the 1998 listing
* '''[[Balboa Pension Fund]] (BPF)''': 5% — institutional anchor investor
* '''[[CIEA]]''': 5% — strategic feedstock supplier
* '''Public float''': approximately 82% — held by institutional and retail investors

